# Vendored/build binaries under include/ (and anywhere else they land):
# never text-convert these on checkout. Without this, a Windows clone
# with core.autocrlf=true relies entirely on git's own content-sniffing
# to treat them as binary -- usually correct, but not guaranteed for
# every file shape, and a corrupted DLL/so fails silently (LoadLibrary/
# dlopen just refuses to load it, with no hint that the bytes changed).
*.dll -text
*.so -text
*.dylib -text
*.a -text
*.lib -text
*.exp -text
*.sig -text
*.pdb -text
*.ilk -text

# POSIX shell scripts: keep LF everywhere, not just on the platforms
# that default to it. demo/run_demo.sh and docker/demo-entrypoint.sh
# are COPYd verbatim into the demo image by docker/Dockerfile -- a
# CRLF-converted checkout would break them inside the container
# (/bin/sh rejects \r-terminated lines), the same way it would break
# the run_build_test.sh bind-mount path.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows batch files: the mirror image. cmd.exe misparses LF-only batch
# files by tokenizing lines at arbitrary byte offsets (mid-word command
# fragments, ALINK errors from half-read lines), so force CRLF on every
# checkout -- an editor or tool that normalizes these to LF breaks
# scripts\windows\build.bat and build-msi.bat silently.
*.bat text eol=crlf
